00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.270
From Instagram, Gregory Hin asks.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.100
What is the main thing that drives you

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.203
to continue making videos
free for all students?

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.900
- Oh, well, you know, I think maybe,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.000
maybe it's somewhat obvious,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.990
but I'll answer the question anyway.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.240
And to be clear, Khan
Academy is much, much more

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.110
than me now, we're over
200 full time folks.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.040
There's thousands of
volunteers around the world,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.010
who make Khan Academy the work.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.650
And I think I'm speaking for everyone,

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.220
including the many thousands,
actually tens of thousands

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:35.510
of hundreds of thousands of people

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.180
who've donated to Khan Academy.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.830
Many of you, I think the,
the, the need to society

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.410
of a world where anyone on the planet

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.660
is able to tap into
their potential, I think.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.120
It's, it's huge.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.380
If you look at almost any
other problem in society,

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.520
whether you're talking
about obviously healthcare

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.210
is a topic these days,
but if you're talking

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.000
about economics, if you talk about safety,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.530
if you really keep peeling
the onion on one way

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.830
or the other, it does
boil down to education.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.840
And if you look at, you
know, access to education

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.170
and quality of education and
what people's living conditions

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.870
are in the world, there's
definitely a strong correlation

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.020
and they obviously feed into each other.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.950
So I personally, I can't
imagine, I consider myself

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:24.090
the luckiest person on the
planet that I get to devote

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.770
as many as, as much of
my energies as possible

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.010
to this problem or to the
solution of empowering folks.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.130
I think it's just a very,
very powerful multiplier.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.180
You know, as famous that
you can give people fish,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.520
but you can also teach them to fish

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.770
and that's what education is really doing.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.090
So that's what drives me day in, day out.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.670
There is another, I would say
a layer, a selfish motivation,

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:49.290
if you will, which is I
love the creation aspect

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.870
and I love, you know, all of
these topics that we cover

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.200
on Khan Academy are
really beautiful topics.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.580
And oftentimes I think,
students feel like, Oh, I gotta

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.190
go through this and you know,
when am I gonna use this?

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.580
But I think there's a,
there's a joy that we hope

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.580
we can bring to students
and teachers and parents,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.880
to realize that this is inherently
beautiful subject matter.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.090
And it's fun to dive in deep with it.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:18.214
So, anyway, I'm incredibly
inspired day in day out

00:06:18.214 --> 00:06:20.350
to be able to serve all of y'all.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.310
And I think everyone, I speak for everyone

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.250
on our Khan Academy team, all
of the thousands of volunteers

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.100
and the many people who've
donated like, like yourselves.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.760
- So, so another one from Instagram.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.820
This one, Moogy asks, how is Khan Academy

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.860
planning to help AP students
study for online AP exams?

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.830
Actually where we're working
on getting college board

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.160
to join you on this homeroom

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.373
and some folks internally as well.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.610
- Yeah, no, that's a
very relevant question.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.860
For those of you who don't
know, you know, the AP,

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.720
which are these advanced placement tests

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.490
in the United States and
some parts of the rest

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.850
of the world, these are
essentially advanced high school

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.440
or intro college level
classes that are taken

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.710
at the high school level.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.240
And at the end of the
year you traditionally

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:06.820
have something called an AP exam.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.570
And depending how a student
scores from one to five,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.640
they could get various
levels of college credit

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.920
depending on where they,
where they go to college.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.930
And this year, testing
globally has become difficult

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.840
because it's not clear that
school will be in session

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.290
during the test, AP tests
are normally in April,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.770
timeframe late April or early May.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.300
And, obviously, other forms of tests

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.370
are also a lot of question marks.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.720
So the college board has announced

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.330
that they're going to do an
online version of the AP,

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.350
which is going to be
different from previous years.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.270
And what we are doing
is we are coordinating,

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.270
we're, we're close friends
with the college board.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:41.460
That's another not-for-profit.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.130
They're the folks who administer
the SAT and the AP exams.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.510
Khan Academy has always had
the official practice partner

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:51.420
around the SAT, but we also
have a lot of AP content

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.950
around calculus, statistics,
biology, chemistry, physics,

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.630
microeconomics and
macroeconomics, American history,

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:01.350
AP CS principles and civics in government

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.550
or American politics.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:06.150
So, what we're going to
do is continue to try

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.650
to improve the resources that we have.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.730
And as we get closer to the AP exam,

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:12.360
we're gonna think about
partnering with college board

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.443
to provide other ways so few people

00:08:14.443 --> 00:08:16.920
feel as prepared as possible.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.670
What my recommendation to
any student who is preparing,

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:25.230
especially if it's a subject
that we have the resources for,

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.170
is try to get as close
to 90 or 100% mastery

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:32.750
on Khan Academy in that AP
subject, in that AP course.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:34.930
And when I say mastery,
you have as much practice

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:36.760
as you need on Khan Academy and you'll,

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:37.980
there's these mastery mechanics

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.370
that will keep leveling you up.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:40.750
You can leverage course challenges

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:43.050
to just understand how well
you know the whole course

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:46.050
at this point you can use
unit tests to break it down

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.420
by unit and then you can keep leveling up

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:49.930
with the, with the various mechanics.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:52.530
And then as you get closer to the AP test,

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:55.300
what I recommend is we do
have worked example videos

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.350
of the free response and the
best way to consume those

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:03.130
is start at the video, look at
the prompt and then pause it.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.750
And I usually do say pause the video

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:06.950
or one of our content
creators say pause a video,

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.300
try to answer it yourself
and then let the video play.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.060
So you get that practice.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.870
And I think if you do those two things,

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.500
you're going to be quite prepared.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:16.810
And we're gonna work
with the college board

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:18.880
to get more information
out because I know this,

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.020
this version of the AP is going
to be a little bit different

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.670
than what we've seen in the past.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.610
So see other questions we have.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:33.140
So from Facebook I see Shala Kareema says,

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.400
my fifth grade son is a smart student,

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.400
but he doesn't challenge himself at all.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.350
He thinks he knows everything.

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:40.663
How can I help him?

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.080
You know, I'm not an expert here

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.670
and I hope in future live
streams we can bring in some,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.937
you know, child psychologists or coaches

00:09:49.937 --> 00:09:52.010
or, or whatever else.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:54.530
My gut sense is, you know,

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.310
this all is about growth
mindset versus fixed mindset.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:58.540
And there is some research showing

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:03.540
how to help coach people to a,
towards a, a growth mindset.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.850
Based on your description,
it sounds like your son

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:09.150
might be falling into a fixed mindset.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:12.010
Now he thinks, his fixed
mindset is, I'm very capable.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:13.300
I know this stuff.

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:17.200
But even if you're a fixed
mindset is one of confidence

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.560
that might actually keep you from engaging

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.330
in things that you find difficult,

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.250
which it sounds like
might be happening here.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.300
Because if you already
have a self perception

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:28.010
that you're very protective
of, that I'm capable,

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:31.340
then you might be afraid to
test that self perception.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:33.650
And so what I would do
is work with your son

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:36.410
as much as possible to
build a growth mindset.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:39.520
Remind your son it's not about
some innate ability level.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.930
It's how much you're
willing to push yourself

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.500
out of your comfort zone and
your brain grows the most

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.880
when you push yourself
out of that comfort zone,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.520
you struggle even fail but
you reflect on that failure

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.330
and pick yourself back
up and history the world.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:58.887
However you wanna define happiness,

00:10:58.887 --> 00:11:02.763
people who are able to, or success,

00:11:02.763 --> 00:11:05.370
people who have a growth mindset,

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:06.840
who are always pushing themselves,

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.190
who don't say I'm smart or dumb,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:11.010
but say, Hey, I just need to
understand what I'm capable of

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.780
by keep pushing myself I embrace failure.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.090
They're going to be
disproportionately successful

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:20.033
regardless of what your
definition of success actually is.

