The foundation series I first
read it in middle school

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.893
and what I found inspiring
about it was, you know,

00:11:58.893 --> 00:12:00.700
it's a story, it takes place 30,

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.410
I'm not giving away any of the plot line.

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:05.010
It takes away, it takes place
30,000 years in the future.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.930
Humanity has colonized the galaxy.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:11.030
There's a galactic empire
and there's this professor

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:15.340
named Harry Seldon who sees
that and he, he, he, he studies

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.700
a combination, it's a
new field of mathematics,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.940
statistics, economics, history.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:22.500
And he's able to statistically predict

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.140
large scale historical movements.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:29.410
And he sees with his mathematics that the,

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:32.260
there's a large probability
that the galactic empire

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.040
is about to enter into dark ages,

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.150
the dark ages that will last 10,000 years.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.130
And so he decides to
do something about it.

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.070
He wants, and you know, in the dark ages,

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:42.760
the empire will get fragmented.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.510
Knowledge will be lost, you'll
have war, you'll have famine.

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.830
And so he statistically
sees that if he's able

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:52.300
to preserve the knowledge of humanity

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.680
in the periphery of the galaxy someplace,

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.300
he, his math shows him
that he will be able

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:01.280
to shorten that dark ages from
10,000 years to 1000 years.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.030
And, and that's what the foundation is.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:05.750
It's the preservation of knowledge.

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:08.410
And when I read that, when
I was in middle school,

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:10.990
there was a, there was a few
takeaways I got from that.

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:13.460
It was a bit of an aha that if you really

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:18.020
want to preserve the essence
of what makes a civilization

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.010
capable, it really is
knowledge and education.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:24.390
And, and if you really
wanna help society the most,

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.010
there's a lot of other
things that matter as well.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.670
But knowledge and education
are at the root of it.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.450
The other thing that I
found really inspiring,

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:34.550
it was this notion, obviously
it's a science fiction book,

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:39.400
but this notion of thinking
far beyond your direct life,

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:43.040
your direct degeneration or
then your or your direct benefit

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.650
that as human beings we
sometimes need to think

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.710
on a larger scale and
on a longer timescale.

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.190
And obviously Harry Seldon in the book

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.680
is thinking on a thousands
of years timescale.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:56.380
And so when Khan Academy started becoming,

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:57.340
I guess you could say a thing,

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.830
I started tutoring family
members and one thing

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.780
led to another and many of
you all started using it.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.710
You know, there was a
world where Khan Academy

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.220
could have just become an
interesting YouTube channel.

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.910
There is a world where
we could have just become

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:11.860
a software company in a tech company.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:13.300
But there's a world where we would think

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.950
about it a little bit
more like Harry Seldon.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:16.960
And this was delusional for a guy

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:20.500
operating out of a walk in
closet not too long ago,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:21.460
10 years ago?

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.520
But what if it could be a
foundation for the world?

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.560
What if it could be an
institution for the world

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.790
that can help billions of people

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:31.400
tap into their potential for generations.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:35.380
And so, that has directly
impacted my hopes,

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:39.390
my aspirations for what Khan
Academy can be and should be.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.860
And you know, this type
of crisis pinpoints it

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:43.970
cause we're going through
a global crisis right now.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.710
There's 1.4 billion
students around the world

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:49.425
who normally would be in
school but are now not

00:14:49.425 --> 00:14:52.490
and, this thing we're creating,

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.550
Khan Academy has a role to play.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.670
So we're already starting
to hopefully help out a bit.

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:01.140
And then you could imagine
in 50 years, 100 years,

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.370
well beyond, you know, my lifespan,

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.080
I hope that in 100 years, 200 years,

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.950
people would have trouble imagining

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.250
what the world was like before
everyone had free access

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:14.810
to things like this that I
hope they take it for granted

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.176
that anyone, regardless
of where you grew up,

00:15:17.176 --> 00:15:18.620
you're going to be able to have a lifeline

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:22.630
and tap into your potential
and have access to opportunity.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:26.640
So there's other questions here.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:29.240
So one from Instagram, someone
says, how do you earn money?

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:32.530
This is a Arife.ercon on Instagram.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.060
And so the simple answer
is, and I've talked

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.500
about it at different live streams.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.590
Khan Academy is a not
for profit organization,

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:41.250
which means no one owns Khan Academy.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:43.330
I don't own it or you own
as much of it as I do.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:44.750
It's a public charity.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.160
But all of us who work at Khan
Academy, we do get salaries

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.780
and then that, those salaries
come from the donation.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:53.760
So when folks donate to Khan Academy,

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:55.760
some of it goes to our server costs,

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.130
which are even before the crisis,

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:00.240
were running at around
six or 7 million a year.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.820
Our traffic is about 2.5 times that.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.780
So we could see, you know,
10 $15 million a year

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:07.620
of server costs or more.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.060
And then a lot of our budget
is paying the salaries

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:15.190
of the engineers, the
designers, the product managers,

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.390
the content creators, the
folks working with classrooms

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.860
and districts, the folks
running the webinar

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:23.860
to pay all of their
salaries so that we can

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:27.570
create this platform that
you know, now we're seeing,

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.830
you know, tens, many tens
of millions of students,

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.680
you know, tens of millions
were using it before.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.603
And, now it's, it's, it's
increasing dramatically.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:40.180
So other subjects, other
questions and Dan filler

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:44.190
I'm just picking, you
know, randomly from this.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:45.803
So actually I like this
question on Facebook.

00:16:45.803 --> 00:16:48.610
Agam Mathia asks, how
can I help my community

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:49.833
during these times?

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:54.010
You know, I was talking to my
wife about it and you know,

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:56.560
even my mom, you know, I'm,
I'm, I'm kind of worried

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.340
about her she's by herself in New Orleans.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:02.060
And obviously she's,
she's of a higher risk age

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:05.500
and, you know, frankly, just
the loneliness of, you know,

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:08.110
not just living alone, but
now being socially distanced,

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:09.850
but then she has to worry
about how she gonna get

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:11.180
her groceries and things like that.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:13.020
And so I think one potential idea,

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:16.080
especially for you younger
folk who have a lower risk,

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:18.410
and, but even then you should
take extreme precaution

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:21.430
is, you know, find
especially senior citizens

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:25.050
in your community who
might need help getting

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.020
the groceries or things like that.

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:30.550
And, doing it for them you
know, just as a service,

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.130
and, you know, still
take all the precautions,

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:33.730
make sure you have, you
know, try to wear a mask,

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:37.180
whatever you can do to, to
minimize your own exposure.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:39.850
But, if, if your family has the means,

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:43.240
you can buy the groceries and
just leave it on their porch.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:45.460
Or if the senior has the means,

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:48.425
but it's just highly
risky for them to go out.

00:17:48.425 --> 00:17:50.860
You know, for some find
some way that they can

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.510
provide the funds, but then
you can go do the grocery.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:56.470
So it's a very tactical,
very simple thing.

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:57.970
But I think it's very powerful.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.620
I think there's another
layer, which is make sure

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:03.030
that the people in your
community feel connected

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:04.700
in this time of social distance.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:06.530
Obviously people are
worried about the virus,

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:08.570
but the next worry is people are feeling

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:10.090
very lonely right now.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:12.760
And so I think, especially
with a lot of these seniors,

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.620
but I would say anyone in your community

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:16.450
make sure everyone feels
somewhat connected.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:19.810
So that might be a getting on
a video conference with them,

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:21.560
just talking to them about life.

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:23.440
There's ways that we can
connect with each other

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.140
and support each other where
we don't have to be in person.

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.730
Yeah, so those are, those
are two of my ideas.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:31.440
There's other ideas, you know,
I know in a lot of places

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.520
the blood banks are
running low because of it.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:36.230
So if you're young and you're,
you can do it in a safe way.

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:37.230
That's an interesting thing.

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.360
So there's, I'm sure many,
many other opportunities

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:40.863
to, to volunteer.

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:45.650
- So we have a question
from the curious student

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.840
from YouTube, I believe
you're the best teacher,

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.710
but who do you think is or has been

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.210
the best teacher to the world?

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:59.210
- Well, thank you for that kind of remark.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:04.210
It makes me, I think, you
know, there's, I will never

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:08.370
make a superlative comment
in almost any domain,

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.270
especially something like teaching.

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:12.740
I think there's different
types of teaching

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:14.640
and there's, and different
people are going to resonate

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.370
with different folks.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:18.940
You know, I like to believe and thank you

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:21.720
for your compliment that
the things that I create

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.160
or the things that our team
here at Khan Academy creates

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:26.880
resonate with as many people as possible,

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:30.090
that help motivate them, help them learn.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:34.890
But you know, the work that
teachers do day in, day out,

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:37.907
goes far beyond that you
know, that's the work

00:19:37.907 --> 00:19:41.600
of you know, working
with individual students,

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.680
individual classrooms, making
sure they're motivated,

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:48.020
you know, diagnosing an individual student

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:50.100
that, okay, it might be this academic gap

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:51.530
or there may be, there's something at home

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:52.590
that I can help them with.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.600
And so the really amazing teachers I know

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:57.420
are the ones that really
form those connections.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.240
And you know, when I think
about my own childhood

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:03.920
or education, I had many,
many amazing teachers.

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.107
I was very blessed that way.

00:20:06.107 --> 00:20:11.107
I grew up in Louisiana and the,
but you know, many teachers

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:13.240
almost every year, I
can name at least one,

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.070
but, you know, in high
school I had teachers like

00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:17.320
Ms. Kennedy and Mr. Hernandez,

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:21.910
and they were incredible
teachers of their subject area.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:26.560
But what I really remember
is they, they were,

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:30.270
they were very connected
to all of us students

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.000
in a very open way.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.770
They, they treated us, you know, they,

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.810
they put constraints on
us as they should have,

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:39.450
but they also they, they,
they treated us like peers.

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:43.070
I remember Ms. Kennedy
was our journalism mentor

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:46.560
and so, that modeled a lot of, you know,

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.520
what I try emulate now in Khan Academy,

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.970
that Khan Academy should never
feel like we're the teacher.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:52.930
You're the student.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.760
It's really that, you know,
we're just two human beings

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.260
trying to help each other.

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:59.810
And maybe right now we have
a little bit more knowledge

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:01.680
and, you might be the recipient of it,

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:03.740
but it is a little bit
more of a equal thing

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.250
you know, Mr. Hernandez
was my, algebra two teacher

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:09.760
and then he was the mentor for
several clubs that I was in.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:12.130
If I go back to middle
school, I remember miss North,

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:14.410
you know, she used to run
her and I remember Ms. Ellis

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:17.000
in fifth grade, they
both used to run their,

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:21.530
their English classes, like,
like university seminars.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:23.840
And I remember that was really fascinating

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.380
and it informs a lot of
what we now recommend,

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:29.470
for what the best model is
when, when folks get together.

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:32.440
Yes, use Khan Academy if you
need lectures at your own pace,

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:35.900
use Khan Academy to get
a practice and feedback

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:37.320
and to get some of your foundations.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.610
And then ideally that
can free up class time

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.050
to run it the way that
Ms. Kennedy or miss North

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:45.880
or Ms. Ellis ran their classes,
which is more dialogue,

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.610
more simulations, more games that really

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.870
push that higher order thinking.

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:53.270
So anyway, there's a lot of
layers to what makes something

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.160
a great teacher and a lot of
different types of teachers.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.060
But, but thank you for the compliment.

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:03.820
So there's one question that
I'm actually intrigued by.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:05.040
No one's ever asked this before.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.220
This is from Agam again,
asking Khan Academy

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.550
should provide resources
for college essays.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:12.920
So we don't have the resources
for college essays yet

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.980
or, or we don't even
have plans for that yet.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:18.220
We do have some resources
on college admissions.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.840
If you do college admissions Khan Academy,

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.270
do a Google search, you'll find it.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:25.320
But my advice for folks
writing college essays

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:28.380
is just be authentic, just be yourself.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:32.080
I think sometimes folks get
so stressed about things

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:35.170
that they try to optimize it
so it's perfect in some way,

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:37.750
but when you try to
optimize, it's perfect.

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:39.810
You're not letting your true self out.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:41.480
You're not letting your vulnerability out.

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:44.110
And you know, I've talked
to many of our advisors

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:45.760
are actually heads of
admissions departments

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:49.220
at some you know fairly
selective universities.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.020
And they tell me, you know, you can't,

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.550
how many kids just kind
of fit a certain pattern

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:57.720
and they're just trying to
have this perfect essay.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.930
But the ones that really appeal to them

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.700
are the ones where when they read it,

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.050
they're like, Oh, I see the student,

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:06.170
I see the person behind this.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:08.550
This person is quirky,
this person's authentic,

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:09.750
this person's honest.

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:11.740
And those are the folks, once again,

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.050
and they have a growth mindset too.

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.070
They're not trying to make
their life look perfect.

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:16.790
They're willing to admit their failures

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:18.380
and they're owning their failures.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:19.740
Those are the students that they like

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.170
to bring to their community.

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:22.560
Those are the students that they think

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.540
are going to be able to do
amazing things in the future.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:28.400
So that's my, my best advice
is just be as authentic

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:31.550
as possible in your essays,
introspect who you are,

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:33.810
don't try to be, you know, some ideal

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.760
that you think other
people want you to be.

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:38.230
And frankly, that's life
not just for, that's advice

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:40.733
not just for college essays
but probably for life.

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:47.620
- So we have a question on
Facebook from Scott Yang Sao,

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:49.420
and this is on the mind
of lots of students,

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:51.600
Sal, will students have
to restart their courses

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.150
because of the school closures?

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:58.850
- So I think that is, it's
a very fluid situation.

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.650
What we've seen in various districts.

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:05.020
I would be, I would be
shocked if you have to restart

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:08.930
your courses, like if you were
taking high school biology

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:09.763
this year, then all of a sudden

00:24:09.763 --> 00:24:12.410
you're gonna have to retake
high school biology next year.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.320
I don't think anyone is,
you know, in the districts

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.850
that we've been talking to
suggesting that type of thing.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:20.750
I think what will happen,
I know some of our,

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:22.810
the local districts
out here in California,

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.030
they've kind of moved to a
bit of a pass fail system.

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:26.670
So at least you get credit.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:30.050
I think what we're
going to have going into

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.940
the end of this school
year over the summer

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:36.740
and the early next school
year is I think teachers,

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:38.840
schools, districts are
going to be very open

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.210
and colleges are going
to be, college admissions

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:44.100
are going to be open
to alternative evidence

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.930
that you mastered the subject.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:48.520
I don't know what these
are fully going to be.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.400
I do, you know, think that
if you are able to master

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:54.820
something on Khan Academy and
print it out and you know,

00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:58.090
sign a, you know, promise,
you know, so that,

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:00.470
that it's your work on an honor code

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:02.755
and it should really be your work,

00:25:02.755 --> 00:25:05.830
that, that even that might carry weight

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:09.190
cause people are looking
for alternative measures

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:11.540
in this next year and I think
everyone is trying to earn

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:14.710
on the side of doing right by everyone

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:16.440
versus trying to be punitive.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:18.290
But I think that it's a two way street.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.440
Everyone, you know, you're the student.

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:23.550
It's on you to be you
know authentic and honest

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:26.410
and of your true state and not try to game

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:30.250
whatever system gets put in
place over the next few months.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:31.930
But I, I wouldn't feel anxious

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:33.990
about having to completely repeat it.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.040
I think there's ways that you can show

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:39.500
that you have that knowledge,
and that if you're,

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.690
if you're good at
marketing it so to speak,

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:45.460
you will be able to, to do fine.

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:47.560
And you know, when I go
back, think of even my own

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:50.520
high school experience,
obviously I didn't go through

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:52.810
what we're all going through right now,

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:56.280
but there is a certain
power to self-advocacy.

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:59.530
So I remember I wanted to
take some advanced courses

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:02.170
at the university of New
Orleans when I was 15 or 16.

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:06.510
And I remember walking in there,
you know, 14 or 15 year old

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:08.320
and they're like, who are you?

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:09.350
I'm like, I'm the high school kid,

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:10.820
I go to, you know, Grace King High School.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:12.390
It's about four miles away.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:15.520
I'd love to take, you know,
multivariable calculus.

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:17.360
And they're like, we
don't offer that to you,

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:18.510
but I'm like, no, I'm ready.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:20.410
And they're like, well, how
do we know you're ready?

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:22.000
And then I had to self advocate.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.020
I'm like, give me a test, here's this.

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:26.470
And I obviously didn't have
Khan Academy back then.

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:31.450
And I think if you advocate
for yourself, you'll also,

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:34.090
people will, will appreciate that.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:37.193
So I think that's another
way to think about it.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:45.840
So we have more questions
here is so from Instagram,

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:48.800
this is a question we've
gotten several times.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:51.550
What's the best way to
avoid procrastination

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:54.360
and, I don't wanna pretend that I'm immune

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.060
to procrastination,
I've done my fair share

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:58.760
and I think especially
in the context we're in,

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:01.250
where the news is, it
kind of sucks you in.

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:02.420
It's very easy to convince you,

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:03.695
Oh, let me just spend five minutes

00:27:03.695 --> 00:27:05.560
looking at what's going on in the world.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:08.710
And then that can turn into
a longer than five minutes.

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:10.247
What I try to do is actually one thing,

00:27:10.247 --> 00:27:13.470
and I try to limit how much I
look at the news these days.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:16.420
I wanna be informed, but I
don't wanna be sucked into it.

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:19.810
I think the other thing is,
and this is during this crisis

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:24.240
or pre this crisis, just
always have a, some structure

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:25.590
and have a bit of a checklist

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:27.900
and then you can build that
momentum during the day.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:29.740
I've talked to multiple live streams.

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.580
I like to wake my bed
first in the morning.

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:33.980
I like to do a meditation
first thing in the morning.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:35.730
I always get ready first
thing in the morning,

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.440
even if I have nowhere to
go, even if it's a weekend,

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.634
I try to do a little bit
of exercise and that those

00:27:41.634 --> 00:27:44.720
early wins helped me build
momentum into the day.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.440
And once I get into the office
or in and now the new world,

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:49.100
when I just start working from home,

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:51.890
I try to, okay, what are some things

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:54.050
that I can, I can get some wins on.

00:27:54.050 --> 00:27:56.070
The other thing, and this
is the Pomodoro technique

00:27:56.070 --> 00:28:01.000
that we've talked about
which is try to give yourself

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:03.190
some windows to work and then
make sure you take breaks

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:04.670
as well so you don't get burned out.

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:06.060
And what Philippe is showing you,

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:08.080
is we've actually made some meditations

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:10.090
to help you stop procrastinating.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:12.690
And these are meditations
just to kind of a way

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:15.620
to be reflective of your own thoughts.

00:28:15.620 --> 00:28:17.990
But you know, the gist
of the, the meditation,

00:28:17.990 --> 00:28:21.170
the procrastination one is, you know,

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:23.520
don't let your brain convince you.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:25.950
Cause it's oftentimes the
fixed mindset in your brain

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.920
that is afraid to get started.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:30.190
It's afraid to get started
because it might discover

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:31.670
that it's harder than you thought

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:33.360
or you might not finish it in time.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:35.780
And that somewhat self destructively

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:37.440
that fear keeps you from starting

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.040
and it becomes a self fulfilling prophecy.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:40.650
It does become harder though the key

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.860
is at that thought process and say, no,

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:46.220
I don't care what you're
trying to convince me brain,

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:48.800
thoughts, I am just gonna start.

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:51.260
I am just going to put one
foot in front of the other

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:53.490
start and see what happens.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:56.630
And if you do that, then it kind of breaks

00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:59.320
that procrastination
circuit in your brain.

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.400
And then as soon as you
start, most times you're like,

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:03.370
Oh, this isn't so bad,
this is kind of fun.

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:06.045
Whoa, why did I procrastinate so much?

00:29:06.045 --> 00:29:09.250
But that's, that's you
know, there's other games

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:10.280
you can play with yourself

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:11.740
or if you have a due date on something,

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:13.977
just, you know, tell yourself
of the due dates earlier.

00:29:13.977 --> 00:29:15.500
We, we could, we could talk about that,

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:17.160
but I'd love any ideas y'all have as well.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:19.400
It's not like anyone has
solved procrastination.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:22.250
But we have ways of dealing
with it or, or minimizing it.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:28.400
Let's see, there's a
bunch of questions here.

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:33.730
We have some from Facebook.

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:37.300
I'm not Chima says, what do
you think about new programs

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:38.640
in the near future that could impact

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:41.330
a few next few generations?

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:43.810
Well, I, it's a fun
question to think about,

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:45.610
you know, in the
education realm, obviously

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:47.380
I hope Khan Academy is in a position

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:50.370
that we can help billions of
folks for generations to come.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:52.430
And there's things that
I hope we can layer on.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:55.220
I hope one day Khan
Academy, you know, someone's

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:57.680
the question about credit
or having to repeat classes

00:29:57.680 --> 00:29:59.410
allude to something
that I hope Khan Academy

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:02.970
can play a role in, in the
future, which is help people

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.610
connect the learning on Khan Academy

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:06.340
so that they get opportunity in the world

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:07.180
or they get credits.

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:09.910
So, you know, there's a
lot things that we could do

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:11.600
on the Khan Academy layer.

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:15.560
I think the other really
interesting things

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:17.370
that are going on in the,
in the world right now,

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:20.660
obviously we're in the midst
of a healthcare crisis.

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:23.460
But I think in healthcare
you're going to have,

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:24.860
you know, there's CRISPR technology

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:27.820
where we're gonna be able to
start literally editing DNA.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:29.925
There's some scary aspects to that.

00:30:29.925 --> 00:30:33.680
We were in the position
to edit our genome,

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:35.470
but there's, there could
be some very positive,

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.390
powerful things from that as well.

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:40.760
I'm inspired by some of the
things that might be happening

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:45.220
in, in, in, in communications.

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:48.320
Obviously the fact that we can
do this type of communication

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:51.280
right now without a ton of planning.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:54.840
You know, in this time
of crisis can you know,

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:57.480
it's making the whole
world a lot more connected.

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.810
I'm inspired by some of the things

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:01.660
that might be happening in energy.

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:03.450
You know, we're all worried
about what might happen

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.300
in the climate and whatnot.

00:31:05.300 --> 00:31:07.590
But, there's, there are
some silver linings.

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:09.830
There's, you know, people
working on nuclear power plants

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:13.750
that might not have any,
nuclear waste to them

00:31:13.750 --> 00:31:15.760
or radioactive waste to them.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:18.190
Solutions like that I
think are pretty inspiring.

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:19.600
I'm also inspired by, you know,

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:21.400
new models of transportation.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:23.326
People are talking
about ways to, you know,

00:31:23.326 --> 00:31:26.560
flying cars and whatever
else and they, they actually

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:29.300
seem more real than not
obviously, space travel,

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:32.510
reusable rockets, satellite
networks that can give everyone

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:35.700
broadband and connectivity
like we have around the globe.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.520
I think all of these
are gonna be incredibly

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:40.370
transformational and they all
in fact affect each other.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:42.080
And you know, they actually
all would have impact

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:44.483
on what Khan Academy is as well.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:51.110
So let's see, there's
other questions here.

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:55.350
So some from YouTube,
Susanna Dominguez asks,

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.520
what is your favorite
part of Khan Academy?

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:01.613
Well, you know, that's like
asking who's my favorite child?

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:05.220
I don't have (laughs) a favorite.

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:07.610
I will say what I think
is the most powerful part

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:09.760
of Khan Academy you know a lot of people

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:11.900
associate Khan Academy with videos

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:14.070
and we are proud of our videos

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.400
and videos are an important
part of what we do.

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:18.660
But I would say the most
powerful part of Khan Academy

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.940
is the practice and
assessment exercise platform

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:24.780
that we have where students
can get functionally

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:27.190
unlimited practice, immediate feedback.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:30.180
There's mechanics for them to
progress at their own pace.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:33.920
And then all of that
ideally works when teachers

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:35.500
are able to look at
their teacher dashboards

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:37.180
or parents are able to
look at either the parent

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:39.930
or the teacher dashboard
to help unstick students

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:42.570
and then to open up what
can happen in class time

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.060
to be a more higher, higher order.

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:46.870
So I think that's my, that's what I think

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:49.180
is the real power of Khan Academy.

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:52.000
The, the, the, it's nice to
get these micro explanations

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:54.440
if you're stuck, but the
real learning happens

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:58.070
when you try exercises,
you, you get feedback,

00:32:58.070 --> 00:32:59.180
especially when you get it wrong.

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:02.180
And then you reflect, you look
at the step-by-step solution

00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:04.710
and the hints and you're like,
Oh, and then you keep trying.

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:06.970
And then you see it in
a context wishing mode

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:10.090
on a unit test or, or something like that.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.010
So, we are out of time.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:14.580
Y'all asked, you got
me on me on my soap box

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.690
on some of my, I should say
from my favorite questions

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:20.920
of like where could these
be in thousands of years.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:22.460
But, but thank you everyone for joining.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:25.380
I know this is an incredibly
hard time for the world.

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:28.980
I would say just as one human
being to another human being,

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:31.040
the silver lining of
that is that we're all

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:33.410
in this together, this
is a shared experience.

00:33:33.410 --> 00:33:37.052
I remind myself that and
whenever I'm feeling a little,

00:33:37.052 --> 00:33:41.010
isolated and socially
distanced, and I remind myself

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:45.060
that I'm in a much more
fortunate situation than many,

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:47.280
many folks in the world
are, are finding themselves.

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:48.930
So we're all in this together

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:51.941
and I think we're gonna
get through this together,

00:33:51.941 --> 00:33:54.870
and, you know, take care of yourself,

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:56.200
stay healthy, stay safe.

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:58.200
And all of us here at Khan
Academy are going to do

00:33:58.200 --> 00:33:59.720
whatever we can to support you

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:02.900
and please in future live
streams, come on, give us tips,

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:05.490
feedback, ideas for how
we can do that better.

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:06.323
Thank you.

